From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] AML: one NAL walker for the packet scanners

Four places walked a demux packet NAL by NAL, each with its own loop:
the Dolby Vision RPU and HEVC SEI latches and the Blu-ray 3D subtitle
depth parser, over length-prefixed or Annex-B input, and the MVC
parameter set scan; the SEI latch and the depth parser each also had
their own SEI message loop.
They now share AMLForEachNal and AMLForEachSeiMessage, and the NAL and
SEI payload types they test have names. An SEI NAL without emulation
prevention bytes is read in place rather than copied.
Both live in AMLNalWalker.h, which depends on nothing, so the subtitle
depth parser keeps its stand-alone test.

The MVC scan takes the stream's length size as it is: it only runs
behind the bitstream converter, which only opens on avcC extradata, the
same extradata the length size is read from. Its fallback to four bytes
could not be reached.

One behaviour changes: on Annex-B input the last NAL of a packet now
loses its trailing zero bytes before its SEI messages are read, as every
other NAL already did. A NAL never ends in 0x00, so this only affects an
SEI message whose declared size reaches into those zeros: it is now
ignored, as one cut short anywhere else is.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

